###  ✅ Steps to Update .env for SPA Authentication #####

; When you transition from Postman to a React frontend, follow these steps:

; 1️⃣ Create a new SPA Application in Auth0

; Go to Auth0 Dashboard → Applications → Create Application
; Select Single Page Application (SPA)
; Set up the Allowed Callback URLs and Allowed Logout URLs (e.g., http://localhost:3000 for local testing).
; 2️⃣ Update Your .env File with the new SPA credentials

; Get the Client ID and Domain from the new SPA Application.
; Keep the API Audience the same (since the API remains unchanged).
; ini
; Copy
; Edit
; AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=your-new-spa-client-id
; AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET=your-new-spa-client-secret (optional)
; AUTH0_DOMAIN=your-auth0-domain
; AUTH0_AUDIENCE=https://mytasks2do-api
; 3️⃣ Update Your React Frontend to Use Auth0

; Use Auth0 SDK for React to handle login/logout.
; When the user logs in, React will get the Access Token from Auth0.
; React will store the token and send it with every API request (instead of Postman).
; 4️⃣ Backend Authentication Logic Stays the Same

; The backend (advancedAuth.js) will still validate the token the same way.
; No need to change how the API handles authentication – just switch from Postman to React.

